{{- /*
  Windows Terminal settings (Windows only - excluded elsewhere by .chezmoiignore).

  MERGE, not own: Terminal rewrites settings.json itself (on every Settings UI
  save, and whenever it detects a new VS / WSL / PowerShell install, with
  per-machine GUIDs). Owning the whole file made every `dot up` hit chezmoi's
  "changed since chezmoi last wrote it" prompt, which was confirmed live seconds
  after the first apply. This template reads the current file (the context's
  stdin) and forces only the keys below, the same FORCED/merge idea
  as the VS Code settings step in run_onchange_install_packages.ps1.tmpl.

    globals / profiles.defaults  FORCED keys; every other key is left alone
    styled profiles              keys merged ONLY IF that profile exists here
                                 (never creates Ubuntu 20.04 on a box without it).
                                 Matched by guid, or by source for WSL: every
                                 distro (24.04 here, 26.04 on another box, any
                                 future one) gets the same look, no GUIDs to track.
                                 One exception: PowerShell 7's entry is created once
                                 pwsh.exe is installed. Terminal adds it only on its
                                 next launch, so a fresh install's merge never saw it
                                 and PS7 stayed unstyled (#430 Run 7). Terminal's own
                                 guid + source, so its generator merges into ours
    "SSH: <name>" profiles       OWNED: rebuilt from [[data.ssh_hosts]] each
                                 apply, so a removed host loses its tab too
    schemes/themes (by name), actions (by id), keybindings (by id OR keys)
                                 ours replace same-named entries, rest kept

  Two passes per apply, same merge (context: stdin = the current file, ssh_hosts):
    modify_settings.json                          in chezmoi's file pass, which comes
                                                  BEFORE the package installer runs
    run_after_windows-terminal-settings.ps1.tmpl  after it, rendered then, so it sees
                                                  pwsh and the font the install added
  The font is forced only once its files exist (WINDIR\Fonts or the per-user
  LOCALAPPDATA\Microsoft\Windows\Fonts): forcing a face Terminal cannot find popped
  "font not available" in the installer's own window. pwsh is looked for under
  ProgramW6432 (the 64-bit Program Files whatever the caller's bitness; then
  ProgramFiles). These, WINDIR and LOCALAPPDATA come from the environment so tests
  can point them at fixtures - Git Bash rewrites ProgramFiles for every Windows
  child, so that one cannot be faked there.

  Look: Catppuccin Mocha + MesloLGS Nerd Font Mono. Tab color per environment:
    blue = PowerShell 7   orange = WSL   green = SSH -> Mac   red = SSH -> Linux
  The WSL profile re-pins font + scheme on purpose: WSL's generated fragment
  forces "Ubuntu Mono" + the purple Ubuntu scheme, breaking starship glyphs.

  Dropdown: four segments in one order on every machine (PowerShell + psmux, the WSL distros,
  the SSH hosts, Windows PowerShell + Command Prompt), separators between them, the Developer and
  Azure profiles hidden. Ctrl+Shift+1..9 stay Terminal's position chords - see the "Order and groups" comment
  below for why a custom mapping cannot be labelled truthfully.

  Agent keys: Ctrl+Alt+C/X/O/A type claude/codex/opencode/agy + Enter into
  the FOCUSED pane (sendInput), so one chord works in pwsh, WSL zsh, and an
  SSH session alike (the CLI just has to exist there). Add Shift to open it in
  a new split instead; the split starts in the same directory because both
  shells report it (OSC 9;9 - see the pwsh profile and dot_zshrc).

  Windows: the workflow is several long-lived WINDOWS, each with its own tabs and
  panes, parked on specific monitors.
    firstWindowPreference persistedWindowLayout  restores that on launch -
      state.json keeps one entry per window with its tabs AND its position.
    windowingBehavior useNew  every launch is a NEW window. "useExisting" (the
      earlier value here) dropped each launch into the most recent window as a
      tab, which makes a second window impossible to open by hand; `wt -w 0`
      (or -w <name>) still targets an existing window deliberately.
    centerOnLaunch false  centering fights a restored window's saved position.

  tabWidthMode - the enum is exactly "equal" | "titleLength" | "compact"; there is
  no marquee/scrolling tab text in Terminal, long titles just ellipsize.
    titleLength  sizes each tab to its title. The agent CLIs rewrite titles
                 ("[ ! ] Action Required | ... | repo"), so the row sprawled.
    equal        current: every tab keeps a slice, truncated from the END - the CLIs put
                 status FIRST, so "[ ! ]" survives on an inactive tab.
    compact      inactive tabs shrink to their icon. Tidiest, but the
                 per-tab alert is invisible, so attention has to come from the
                 bell instead (bellStyle below flashes window + taskbar).
  Switching is one word here.

  Clipboard (same in the VS Code terminal, see the installers): highlighting
  copies (copyOnSelect) and right-click pastes, in every pane. OpenCode keeps
  mouse capture (wheel scrolling), so there Shift+right-click pastes - Shift
  hands the mouse back to Terminal.

  Alt+Enter is unbound ("id": null) so it reaches the agent CLIs as a newline
  (OpenCode, agy); full screen stays on F11.

  Ctrl+Enter sends a line feed (0x0A, the byte Ctrl+J is), so it is a newline in
  claude, codex, opencode and agy alike, inside psmux too. Left alone, Terminal
  reports Ctrl+Enter as its own key to a CLI that asks for extended keys, and
  Claude Code (2.1.275+) binds that to chat:sendNow - it SENDS. Same rule: VS Code
  (.chezmoitemplates/vscode-keybindings.json) and Ghostty (dot_config/ghostty).

  tmux = true (or a session name) on an ssh_hosts entry makes its profile run
  `ssh -t <host> "tmux new-session -A -s main"`: every tab re-attaches to the
  same remote session, so an agent keeps running across disconnects.

  SSH GUIDs derive from the host name (sha256), so they are stable across
  applies and machines. Output is strict JSON; a hand-added // comment in
  settings.json makes fromJson fail the apply loudly (Terminal never writes
  comments itself).

  psmux profile: always created (this whole file is Windows-only). It runs the
  local tmux twin (dot_config/psmux/psmux.conf) in pwsh panes. startingDirectory
  is forced to %USERPROFILE%: psmux first launched as a bare commandline
  override on a generated profile, landed in C:\Windows\System32, and starship's
  directory scan timed out on every prompt (30 ms default scan_timeout). Teal
  tab - the one Mocha accent no other profile claims.
*/ -}}
{{- $raw := .stdin | trimPrefix "﻿" | trim -}}
{{- $cur := dict -}}
{{- if $raw }}{{ $cur = fromJson $raw }}{{ end -}}

{{- $want := `{
  "globals": {
    "$help": "https://aka.ms/terminal-documentation",
    "$schema": "https://aka.ms/terminal-profiles-schema",
    "theme": "Catppuccin Mocha",
    "firstWindowPreference": "persistedWindowLayout",
    "windowingBehavior": "useNew",
    "centerOnLaunch": false,
    "initialCols": 140,
    "initialRows": 38,
    "copyOnSelect": true,
    "trimPaste": true,
    "tabWidthMode": "equal",
    "tabSwitcherMode": "inOrder",
    "useAcrylicInTabRow": true
  },
  "defaults": {
